WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.364 --> 00:00:15.527
It was almost midnight when Fagin left Nancy.

2
00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:19.588
As the night was piercing cold, he had no temptation to loiter.

3
00:00:20.669 --> 00:00:23.949
A sharp wind scoured the streets and seemed to clear them of people.

4
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.871
Fagin trembled and shivered as every fresh gust drove him on his way.

5
00:00:29.971 --> 00:00:33.232
At the corner of his own street, he fumbled in his pocket for his key.

6
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.842
A dark figure emerged from the shadow of a doorway and glided up to him.

7
00:00:37.862 --> 00:00:41.303
"Fagin!" whispered a voice close to his ear.

8
00:00:41.323 --> 00:00:45.205
"Ah!" The old man quickly stopped and turned around.

9
00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:48.126
"Is that—" "Yes!"

10
00:00:48.606 --> 00:00:49.646
interrupted the stranger.

11
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:52.267
"I have been lingering here for two hours.

12
00:00:53.248 --> 00:00:54.008
Where have you been?"

13
00:00:54.028 --> 00:00:56.749
"On your business, my dear."

14
00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.670
Fagin glanced uneasily at his companion.

15
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.831
"Oh, of course."

16
00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:03.292
The stranger sneered.

17
00:01:04.956 --> 00:01:06.256
"Well, and what's come of it?"

18
00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:08.077
"Nothing good."

19
00:01:09.137 --> 00:01:10.938
The stranger gave Fagin a startled look.

20
00:01:11.918 --> 00:01:12.998
"Nothing bad, I hope?"

21
00:01:14.139 --> 00:01:16.199
Fagin shook his head and started to walk again.

22
00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:21.281
When they arrived in front of Fagin's house, the stranger said he wanted to talk inside.

23
00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:25.302
"My blood is chilled from standing about so long," he added.

24
00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:27.802
"The wind was blowing right through me."

25
00:01:28.903 --> 00:01:33.244
Fagin did not want to let the man into his house, so he muttered something about not having

26
00:01:33.284 --> 00:01:33.724
a fire.

27
00:01:34.764 --> 00:01:37.165
But the stranger repeated his request as a demand.

28
00:01:38.185 --> 00:01:41.387
At last Fagin unlocked the door, and they both went inside.

29
00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:46.189
"It's as dark as a grave in here," said the man, groping forward a few steps.

30
00:01:46.209 --> 00:01:48.329
"Make haste with a light."

31
00:01:48.349 --> 00:01:52.711
"Shut the door," whispered Fagin from the end of the hall.

32
00:01:53.692 --> 00:01:56.293
As he spoke, the door closed with a loud noise.

33
00:01:56.313 --> 00:02:01.355
"That wasn't my doing," said the other man, feeling his way slowly forward.

34
00:02:02.235 --> 00:02:04.436
"The wind blew it closed, or it shut by itself."

35
00:02:05.556 --> 00:02:07.958
Fagin stealthily descended the stairs to the kitchen.

36
00:02:08.859 --> 00:02:13.183
He soon returned with a lighted candle and the news that Toby Crackit and the boys were asleep

37
00:02:13.243 --> 00:02:18.187
below. Beckoning the man to follow him, he led the way upstairs to the second floor.

38
00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:22.511
"We can say the few words we've got to say in here, my dear."

39
00:02:23.452 --> 00:02:24.593
Fagin threw open a door.

40
00:02:24.613 --> 00:02:28.976
"But there are holes in the shutters, and we don't want the neighbors to see a light.

41
00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:31.779
So we'll set the candle on the stairs.

42
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.927
There!" With those words he placed the candle on a flight of stairs opposite the door.

43
00:02:39.669 --> 00:02:44.052
He led the way into the room, which was empty except for a broken armchair and an old

44
00:02:44.112 --> 00:02:48.636
couch. The stranger sat down on the couch with the air of a weary man.

45
00:02:49.857 --> 00:02:52.579
Fagin drew up the armchair so they sat face-to-face.

46
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.942
The candle threw a feeble light on the opposite wall.

47
00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.805
They spoke for some time in whispers.

48
00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:02.708
The stranger appeared to be in a state of considerable irritation.

49
00:03:03.836 --> 00:03:08.417
"I tell you again, the robbery was badly planned," the man said, raising his voice a little.

50
00:03:08.437 --> 00:03:11.818
"Why didn't you keep Oliver here and make him a pickpocket?"

51
00:03:11.838 --> 00:03:15.839
"I couldn't have done that even if I'd wanted to, my dear Monks."

52
00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.340
For it was indeed Monks whom Fagin was addressing.

53
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.801
"Haven't you turned dozens of other boys into pickpockets?"

54
00:03:23.201 --> 00:03:24.302
Monks demanded sternly.

55
00:03:24.322 --> 00:03:28.563
"If you were patient for a year at most, you could get Oliver convicted.

56
00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:33.344
Then he'd be sent safely out of the country, perhaps for life."

57
00:03:34.568 --> 00:03:38.211
"I saw it was not easy to train him in the business," the old man explained.

58
00:03:39.112 --> 00:03:41.814
"He was not like other boys in the same circumstances."

59
00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:43.816
"Curse him, no!

60
00:03:44.797 --> 00:03:47.339
Or he would have been a thief long ago," muttered Monks.

61
00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:53.805
"I had no hold upon Oliver to make him worse," continued Fagin, anxiously watching the face of his

62
00:03:53.845 --> 00:03:59.349
companion. "I had nothing to frighten him with, which I always must have in the beginning or I

63
00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:00.250
labor in vain.

64
00:04:01.391 --> 00:04:02.092
What could I do?

65
00:04:03.056 --> 00:04:04.817
Send him out with the Dodger and Charley?

66
00:04:05.658 --> 00:04:06.999
We saw how well that worked.

67
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.141
I trembled in fear for us all when he was caught."

68
00:04:10.161 --> 00:04:13.684
"That was not my doing," observed Monks.

69
00:04:14.904 --> 00:04:15.905
"No, no, my dear.

70
00:04:17.586 --> 00:04:22.730
And I don't quarrel with it now because if it had never happened, you might never have spotted

71
00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:23.790
him when he was arrested.

72
00:04:24.611 --> 00:04:26.692
But I got him back for you by using Nancy.

73
00:04:27.593 --> 00:04:29.474
And then she began to grow fond of him."

74
00:04:30.555 --> 00:04:31.556
"Get rid of the girl!"

75
00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:32.941
Monks said impatiently.

76
00:04:32.961 --> 00:04:35.622
"That sort of thing is not our way.

77
00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.783
But don't worry," Fagin added with a smile.

78
00:04:38.803 --> 00:04:41.644
"I know how these girls are, Monks.

79
00:04:42.684 --> 00:04:46.746
As soon as the boy begins to harden, Nancy will care no more for him than for a

80
00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:47.546
block of wood.

81
00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:49.787
You want him turned into a thief.

82
00:04:50.768 --> 00:04:52.889
If he's alive, I can do it this time."

83
00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:55.210
Fagin drew nearer to Monks.

84
00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:00.792
"But if worst comes to worst and he's dead "It's no fault of mine if he is!"

85
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.101
Monks grasped Fagin's arm with trembling hands and a look of terror.

86
00:05:05.121 --> 00:05:07.743
"Be careful, Fagin.

87
00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:10.964
I told you from the first that I won't shed blood.

88
00:05:11.904 --> 00:05:12.885
Anything but his death!

89
00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:16.826
If he was shot dead in that robbery, I was not the cause."

90
00:05:17.807 --> 00:05:18.527
Monks paused.

91
00:05:18.547 --> 00:05:19.988
"What's that?"

92
00:05:21.128 --> 00:05:23.429
"What?" cried Fagin as he sprung to his feet.

93
00:05:23.449 --> 00:05:26.150
"Where?" "There!"

94
00:05:26.971 --> 00:05:28.431
Monks pointed to the opposite wall.

95
00:05:28.451 --> 00:05:29.732
"A shadow!

96
00:05:30.632 --> 00:05:32.814
I saw the shadow of a woman in a cloak and bonnet."

97
00:05:33.814 --> 00:05:34.915
They rushed from the room.

98
00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:40.459
The candle, still standing where it had been placed, showed them only the empty staircase and their own

99
00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:41.260
white faces.

100
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.123
They listened intently, but a profound silence reigned throughout the house.

101
00:05:46.143 --> 00:05:51.387
"It's your imagination," said Fagin, taking up the candle and turning to Monks.

102
00:05:52.648 --> 00:05:55.309
"I swear I saw a shadow," replied Monks, trembling.

103
00:05:55.329 --> 00:06:00.456
"It was bending forward when I first saw it, and when I spoke, it darted away."

104
00:06:01.497 --> 00:06:03.297
Fagin glanced contemptuously at Monks.

105
00:06:03.317 --> 00:06:05.518
"Follow me, if you please."

106
00:06:06.618 --> 00:06:08.539
They ascended the stairs to the third floor.

107
00:06:09.439 --> 00:06:13.320
They looked into all the rooms, but each one was cold, bare, and empty.

108
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.981
They descended to the first floor and then to the kitchen below.

109
00:06:18.021 --> 00:06:21.902
The green mold hung upon the low walls, but all was still as death.

110
00:06:23.043 --> 00:06:24.003
"What do you think now?"

111
00:06:24.423 --> 00:06:27.284
asked Fagin after they had returned to the hall by the front door.

112
00:06:28.268 --> 00:06:32.011
"Besides ourselves, there's not a creature in the house except Toby and the boys."

113
00:06:33.171 --> 00:06:36.774
Monks shook his head and laughed grimly, saying it must have been his imagination.

114
00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:41.797
Then he declined to continue talking about Oliver because it was now past one o'clock.

115
00:06:42.718 --> 00:06:44.819
And so he went out into the cold night.
